event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0260d537ca622649006a6cfd9bf6d8819087589340d978928a","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":350,"text":"Using Bing grounding, the latest notable updates on NASA’s Mars Perseverance rover are:\n\n- **Perseverance has driven a full marathon on Mars.** NASA reported that the rover reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on **Sol 1,890**, about **five years and four months** after landing. That is much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to reach the same distance. The milestone was captured from orbit by the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter’s HiRISE camera, showing Perseverance and its tracks on the Martian surface【5:0†source】.\n\n- **The rover is operating west of Jezero Crater**, in a region the science team has nicknamed **“Arbot.”** This comes after Perseverance explored Jezero’s delta region, Neretva Vallis, and climbed to the crater rim in late 2024【5:3†source】.\n\n- **Recent science from the crater rim points to ancient impact history.** NASA’s Mars news page highlights a Perseverance update saying the rover found evidence that a **245-foot / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** on Jezero Crater’s rim was built by repeated asteroid impacts【5:4†source】.\n\n- **Fresh rover imagery is still being released.** NASA’s Perseverance image archive shows recent “Image of the Week” selections for late July 2026, indicating the rover continues returning new surface images from Mars【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, has passed a major driving milestone, is studying rocks beyond Jezero Crater’s rim, and is continuing to send back new images and science data."}

